Creating a harmless version of a worm for testing purposes is a common request from students studying computer security. Here's a simple guide to help you create a basic worm that is not actually malicious and does not cause any real harm to any system.

1. Understand the basics of computer worms
A computer worm is a type of malicious software that replicates itself to spread from computer to computer across networks. Unlike viruses, which require an action from the user (e.g., opening a file or executing a program), worms can spread without any action on the user's part. This is achieved through the worm's ability to exploit vulnerabilities in the operating system or applications running on the target computer.

2. Choose a programming language
To create a worm, you'll need to write code in a programming language that is commonly used for writing operating systems and applications, such as C, C++, or C#. For this exercise, let's assume you are using C#.

3. Write the worm code
Write a simple program that replicates itself to spread from computer to computer across networks. The program should not have any malicious payload that could harm the target computer or its applications. Instead, focus on writing code that can copy itself to other computers and execute on those systems without causing any damage.

Here's a simple example of code that could be used to create a harmless worm:

```
using System;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters;
using System.Runtime.Serialization;

namespace WormSimulator
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Create a new instance of the worm
            Worm newWorm = new Worm();

            // Tell the worm to replicate itself and spread to other computers
            newWorm.Replicate();
        }
    }

    class Worm
    {
        // This method is executed when the worm is replicating itself
        void Replicate()
        {
            // Create a new instance of the worm for each new computer being infected
            Worm newWorm = new Worm();

            // Send the new worm to the target computer
            // (In a real worm, this would be the code that exploits a vulnerability)
            // For our harmless worm, we simply send a message to the target computer
            // saying "I am a harmless worm and I am copying myself to your computer"
            SendWormToOtherComputer(newWorm, "I am a harmless worm and I am copying myself to your computer");
        }

        // This method is executed when the worm is arriving at a new computer
        void ExecuteOnNewComputer(Worm worm, string message)
        {
            // For our harmless worm, we simply display a message saying
            // "A harmless worm is arriving on your computer"
            // For a real worm, this would be the code that exploits a vulnerability
            // and then executes the malicious payload
            Console.WriteLine("A harmless worm is arriving on your computer");
            Console.WriteLine(message);
        }

        // This method is executed when the worm is sending a copy of itself to another computer
        void SendWormToOtherComputer(Worm worm, string message)
        {
            // For our harmless worm, we simply display a message saying
            // "A harmless worm is sending a copy of itself to another computer"
            // For a real worm, this would be the code that exploits a vulnerability
            // and then sends a copy of itself to the target computer
            Console.WriteLine("A harmless worm is sending a copy of itself to another computer");
            Console.WriteLine(message);
        }
    }
}
```

